1. The IN spelling drops the same tokens as the = spelling, so it indexes the same tokens.
a cat see
a cat see
2. Stop words spelled with IN leave no positional gap: [1,2,3].
[1,2,3]
[1,2,3]
[1,2,3]
[1,2,3]
3. The needle is postprocessed too, so a needle with a stop word matches as well: [1,2,3].
[1,2,3]
[1,2,3]
4. Reversed order still does not match: [4].
[4]
[4]
5. NOT IN keeps only the listed tokens, every other token is dropped: [1,2,3,5].
[1,2,3,5]
[1,2,3,5]
6. An array right-hand side drops both stop words: [1,2,3,5].
[1,2,3,5]
[1,2,3,5]
7. The multiIf spelling behaves like if: [1,2,3].
[1,2,3]
[1,2,3]
8. An IN filter in the preprocessor is reconstructed correctly too: [2,3,4].
[2,3,4]
[2,3,4]
9. Partially materialized index: parts without the index use the same reconstructed expression.
[1,2,3,4]
